/* Bizzing India — THE RAMAYANA, serialised.
   docs/10 §6: "the single most valuable content object in the product."

   Shape:
     window.IND_EPIC_RAMAYANA = {
  // The Hindi telling below is a DRAFT: it needs a named Hindi pedagogue per line
  // before launch (docs/05 6). The audio is synthesised from these lines, so a
  // correction means re-running tools/tts.py for that one clip.
  needs_native_speaker: true, id, title, subtitle, blurb, badge, avatar,
                                  books[], episodes[] }
     episode = { n, book, title, hook, minutes, cast[], cards[], ends_on,
                 words_hi[3], note?, gate? }
     card    = { who, text }   who = an avatar id, 'mithu', or null for narration

   THIS IS A LIBRARY, NOT A LADDER (docs/10 §3.5). Nothing in this file scores,
   grades, passes, fails or measures a child. There are no `ask` beats and no
   `moral` field — the deck's only verbs are "next" and "again". Episode numbers
   are the order a storyteller tells them in, not a progress bar.

   STAGING (docs/05). The art set has avatars for Rama and Hanuman and for animals,
   and none for Sita, Lakshmana, Dasharatha, Kaikeyi, Bharata, Ravana, Jatayu,
   Shabari, Sugriva, Vibhishana or Mandodari. Following data-saga.js, those
   characters are staged in narration with their own real dialogue rather than
   played by a borrowed Mughal courtier — a wrong face is worse than no face.
   Two animal avatars are borrowed the way data-stories-regional.js borrows
   pt_mouse for the squirrel: pt_crow stands in for the great birds Jatayu and
   Sampati, pt_deer for the golden deer, pt_monkey for the vanaras.

   EDITORIAL
   · Badge katha throughout, and told with love — this is a story as it is told.
   · Regional variety is not a footnote here, it is the truth of the epic. Where a
     beat comes from a particular telling rather than from Valmiki (the line in the
     dust, the floating stones, the shadow-Sita, Lakshmana at Ravana's deathbed),
     the card says so plainly and does not adjudicate.
   · Ravana is a scholar and a king who does a terrible thing. Mandodari and
     Vibhishana both tell him, to his face, that he is wrong.
   · Sita argues, refuses, chooses and answers back. She is never scenery, and she
     is never carried out of Lanka against her own decision.
   · Ep 23 (the fire) and ep 24 (the Uttara Kanda) are told honestly rather than
     skipped or explained away. Ep 24 carries gate: 10 and a note saying plainly
     that families differ and that stopping at the lamps is a real way to tell it.
   · No violence beyond fairy-tale level. The war is courage and loss.
   · No invented scripture. Every line of dialogue here is retold storytelling and
     is never presented as a quotation from any text.
*/

